1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Alaska. It's unlike any other state in the U.S.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
A vast frozen wilderness stretching from above the Arctic Circle

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
over 1,400 miles south. It's America's

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
last frontier. Big, rugged, and cold.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
60 below zero. Dark out. We come to work in the dark, we go home in the dark.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
It's always dark. Even surviving here takes an arsenal of unique

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
tools, engineered by Alaskans, to take on this ultimate state of extremes.

8
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,000
There are Alaska solutions for Alaska problems.

9
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:33,000
If you didn't have an Alaska as a test ground,

10
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:35,000
then you would have invented one.

11
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:38,000
It's Alaska Tech, now on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
As a land of extremes, Alaska delivers in a big way.

13
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:00,000
On size alone, it tops the charts.

14
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:02,500
Twice the square footage of Texas,

15
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:06,000
with 33,000 miles of shoreline.

16
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:10,000
The state could stretch all the way from Florida to California.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Its landscape makes for epic forces of nature

18
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:18,000
and the coldest temperatures in the US,

19
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:21,000
down to 80 below zero in some parts.

20
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:25,000
39 mountain ranges and over half of the world's glaciers

21
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:28,140
of the world's glaciers separate communities into the most

22
00:01:28,141 --> 00:01:31,540
isolated in the country.

23
00:01:31,541 --> 00:01:35,380
In most of the state, there were only two seasons, 10 months

24
00:01:35,381 --> 00:01:39,240
of winter and two months of thaw.

25
00:01:39,241 --> 00:01:41,040
Both of them wreak havoc on the roads.

26
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:50,500
Alaska's highway system serves just a third of the state,

27
00:01:50,501 --> 00:01:53,700
with only 5,600 miles of roads.

28
00:01:53,701 --> 00:01:55,540
That's the same amount as in Vermont,

29
00:01:55,541 --> 00:01:58,800
yet Alaska is 62 times bigger.

30
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:02,240
No road is more critical than this north-south lifeline,

31
00:02:02,241 --> 00:02:05,640
the Richardson Highway, which brings oil and supplies

32
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:08,240
from the southern coast into Alaska's interior.

33
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:15,460
But this highway is also home to Thompson Pass,

34
00:02:15,461 --> 00:02:20,260
a 2,800-foot gap in the Chugash Mountains, which just so happens

35
00:02:20,261 --> 00:02:23,700
to be the snowiest place in Alaska.

36
00:02:23,701 --> 00:02:30,500
The all-time record for snowfall here, 974 inches.

37
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:35,740
And its yearly average is over 500 inches, more than 41 feet,

38
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,640
enough to bury a four-story building.

39
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:48,460
Then there are the avalanches, which average 20 per year.

40
00:02:48,461 --> 00:02:54,340
It wasn't until 1950 that snowplows even attempted

41
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:56,140
to keep the pass open year round.

42
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:05,820
But ever since, a specialized team of DOT drivers

43
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:11,720
rotates through 20-hour shifts, facing whiteouts day and night.

44
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:14,060
We do our best to keep the roads open,

45
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,280
but sometimes it's just really nasty and, you know,

46
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:19,280
and Alaskans are tough.

47
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,980
You can't tell them it's bad and expect them to go home.

48
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:26,900
So how can these drivers keep plowing

49
00:03:26,901 --> 00:03:28,360
through such treacherous conditions?

50
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:34,000
With a new groundbreaking GPS navigation system

51
00:03:34,001 --> 00:03:37,100
that allows them to steer through a virtual moving map

52
00:03:37,101 --> 00:03:37,801
of the highway.

53
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:44,000
Our truck's antenna communicates with a fixed ground antenna

54
00:03:44,001 --> 00:03:46,340
to determine the truck's position on the road

55
00:03:46,341 --> 00:03:49,380
within three centimeters.

56
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:52,080
The GPS is really like having a sixth sense.

57
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:54,080
When your vision goes away, you still

58
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:56,880
have the GPS to show you where you are.

59
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:58,480
The map appears on a monitor

60
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:01,580
in the back of the cab, which is reflected onto an acrylic

61
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,200
screen, kind of like a see-through video game

62
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:05,300
monitor.

63
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:08,260
This way, drivers can watch the screen and the road

64
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:11,400
at the same time.

65
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:15,140
The driver's truck appears as a white square on the screen.

66
00:04:15,141 --> 00:04:18,240
The center line, intersections, mile markers, turn lanes,

67
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:23,780
guardrails, and avalanche zones are all marked.

68
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:28,680
The way the system works is if I start to move too far to the

69
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:31,020
left and cross over the center line,

70
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:33,300
the yellow center line will turn red,

71
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:35,460
letting me know that I've gone too far to the left.

72
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:37,800
And the same thing if I go too far to the right,

73
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,760
the white fog line will turn red,

74
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,940
letting me know I'm going too far to the right.

75
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:43,700
The GPS unit is also equipped

76
00:04:43,701 --> 00:04:45,740
with other warning sensors.

77
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,340
We also have a seat function I can turn on,

78
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:51,440
which will vibrate the left side of my seat

79
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:53,780
as I travel over the center line.

80
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:55,880
And again, if I go too far to the right

81
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,580
and start to travel over the fog line,

82
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,380
it will vibrate the right side of my seat.

83
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:05,460
Too far to the left at the wrong moment

84
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:10,360
could mean a head-on collision with a semi.

85
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:13,200
But radar enables the system to pick up the location

86
00:05:13,201 --> 00:05:16,200
of other vehicles, so drivers know when to make room

87
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:18,540
for a truck that's about to pass,

88
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:21,940
and when to slow down for an obstruction ahead.

89
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:24,840
It's a major advancement in reducing driver's stress

90
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:26,680
and fatigue on the job.

91
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:29,080
These trucks are basically one-man armies.

92
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:31,520
They really have reduced the amount of time

93
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:34,180
that we have to spend in our graders removing snowpack,

94
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:36,320
and they're really an impressive vehicle.

95
00:05:36,321 --> 00:05:39,320
Plow trucks do their part to keep the roads open

96
00:05:39,321 --> 00:05:41,320
throughout Alaska's winters.

97
00:05:41,321 --> 00:05:43,320
But keeping your own car on the road

98
00:05:43,321 --> 00:05:46,320
is a whole other battle.

99
00:05:46,321 --> 00:05:49,320
Most cars are simply not built to operate at temperatures

100
00:05:49,321 --> 00:05:51,320
down to 60 below.

101
00:05:51,321 --> 00:05:53,320
And the danger of breaking down in these conditions

102
00:05:53,321 --> 00:05:55,320
can't be overestimated.

103
00:05:55,321 --> 00:05:57,320
You're fighting the cold weather.

104
00:05:57,321 --> 00:05:59,320
You're fighting your vehicle.

105
00:05:59,321 --> 00:06:01,320
You know, we got a saying that you don't know what

106
00:06:01,321 --> 00:06:02,320
you're doing.

107
00:06:02,321 --> 00:06:04,320
You're fighting your vehicle.

108
00:06:04,321 --> 00:06:06,820
You know, we got to sing Mother Nature

109
00:06:06,821 --> 00:06:08,320
because she can be a frigid bitch up here.

110
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:12,660
Ken Boschert runs his car shop out of Fairbanks,

111
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:15,260
where everyone knows he's the man to see

112
00:06:15,261 --> 00:06:16,640
about winterizing a vehicle.

113
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:20,240
Because here, even a daily commute

114
00:06:20,241 --> 00:06:22,520
can quickly become a matter of life and death.

115
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:25,220
Buddy and I went to the gym,

116
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,640
broke down on his way home from the gym.

117
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:28,660
He's still in his shorts.

118
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:30,520
He goes to walk home, he makes it,

119
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:33,820
but he frostbit his foot, and he actually lost a toe.

120
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:36,860
So now he's got four tails on one foot.

121
00:06:36,861 --> 00:06:38,960
Simple thing, just driving home from the gym,

122
00:06:38,961 --> 00:06:41,520
50 below one night.

123
00:06:41,521 --> 00:06:42,820
It can happen.

124
00:06:42,821 --> 00:06:46,060
Every year, Ken spends the summer outfitting cars

125
00:06:46,061 --> 00:06:48,860
with Alaska-tested cold-fighting kits.

126
00:06:48,861 --> 00:06:51,980
The first challenge would be to actually just get the car running.

127
00:06:51,981 --> 00:06:53,480
You've got to get started every morning.

128
00:06:53,481 --> 00:06:56,680
And it's 60 below zero, batteries don't work.

129
00:06:56,681 --> 00:06:58,620
And so if you don't have your car winterized

130
00:06:58,621 --> 00:06:59,880
with a whole bunch of heaters on it,

131
00:06:59,881 --> 00:07:01,680
it's not going to do nothing.

132
00:07:01,681 --> 00:07:03,420
So we got one of these four-way boxes,

133
00:07:03,421 --> 00:07:06,720
It plugs into your house on this end.

134
00:07:06,721 --> 00:07:08,620
And what we do is we mount this under the hood

135
00:07:08,621 --> 00:07:10,920
in a nice place so it doesn't get dirty or wet.

136
00:07:10,921 --> 00:07:12,920
And then these are silicone pad heaters.

137
00:07:12,921 --> 00:07:14,460
They do different jobs.

138
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:19,260
The silicone pads are glued onto specific parts

139
00:07:19,261 --> 00:07:21,880
of the engine to keep it warm.

140
00:07:21,881 --> 00:07:24,780
Here is a 100-watt silicone pad heater,

141
00:07:24,781 --> 00:07:27,580
and it's glued right onto the oil pan.

142
00:07:27,581 --> 00:07:29,780
All right, here we see the cord coming up front.

143
00:07:29,781 --> 00:07:32,420
Glue it right on the automatic transmission pan,

144
00:07:32,421 --> 00:07:34,080
and you can put it in gear, and the transmission

145
00:07:34,081 --> 00:07:36,560
actually lets the vehicle move.

146
00:07:36,561 --> 00:07:39,260
One that's a 600-watt, it's a freeze plug heater,

147
00:07:39,261 --> 00:07:40,860
and it heats all the coolant in the engine.

148
00:07:40,861 --> 00:07:42,760
This is the one that does most of the work,

149
00:07:42,761 --> 00:07:44,360
and it just gets blistering hot.

150
00:07:44,361 --> 00:07:46,160
This little heater element fits right in the,

151
00:07:46,161 --> 00:07:49,480
just fits right in the coolant jacket and bolts in.

152
00:07:49,481 --> 00:07:52,280
When all four of Ken's heaters are working properly,

153
00:07:52,281 --> 00:07:54,580
they fool the engine into thinking that it's operating

154
00:07:54,581 --> 00:07:57,680
at a temperature of about 50 above zero.

155
00:07:57,681 --> 00:08:00,320
But once the car is running, it still has obstacles

156
00:08:00,321 --> 00:08:02,420
to contend with on the road.

157
00:08:02,421 --> 00:08:03,880
You need some great tires like these.

158
00:08:03,881 --> 00:08:06,260
These are great tires, big tires.

159
00:08:06,261 --> 00:08:07,920
But if you notice in here, it's all these,

160
00:08:07,921 --> 00:08:09,360
see all these little cuts?

161
00:08:09,361 --> 00:08:10,960
This is called siping.

162
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:14,060
And these little cuts get you extra traction on the ice

163
00:08:14,061 --> 00:08:16,660
because they'll expand out, and they'll just,

164
00:08:16,661 --> 00:08:18,780
they'll actually just take a little bite of that ice,

165
00:08:18,781 --> 00:08:20,900
and they'll pull you along, and help keep your steering

166
00:08:20,901 --> 00:08:23,380
and braking good so you can stay on the road.

167
00:08:23,381 --> 00:08:25,680
As if the cold and ice weren't enough.

168
00:08:25,681 --> 00:08:27,740
Remember, it's dark out all the time.

169
00:08:27,741 --> 00:08:28,820
It's just dark.

170
00:08:28,821 --> 00:08:30,980
We come to work in the dark, we go home in the dark.

171
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:32,420
It's always dark.

172
00:08:32,421 --> 00:08:34,380
And that's not all drivers face.

173
00:08:34,381 --> 00:08:37,860
The moose in Alaska are the biggest of their species.

174
00:08:37,861 --> 00:08:41,820
And every year, 400 to 500 get killed in car accidents,

175
00:08:41,821 --> 00:08:43,600
taking some people out with them.

176
00:08:43,601 --> 00:08:44,800
So it's great to have moose lights.

177
00:08:44,801 --> 00:08:47,240
Big safety features, not just to look cool.

178
00:08:47,241 --> 00:08:48,640
They actually have a purpose.

179
00:08:48,641 --> 00:08:52,980
They look cool, but it's a safety issue.

180
00:08:52,981 --> 00:08:54,740
Keeping your truck running is important

181
00:08:54,741 --> 00:08:55,980
in the towns and cities.

182
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:06,100
But the majority of the state is not even accessible by roads.

183
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:09,880
So to really stay mobile through the winters up here,

184
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:12,620
you've got to winterize your plane.

185
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,400
There are things that Alaskans know very well.

186
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:17,500
Snow is one of them. Flying on snow is another.

187
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:20,740
And that requires landing on snow.

188
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:25,320
If I have skis on, the world is my runway in the middle of winter

189
00:09:25,321 --> 00:09:27,300
if I have skis. I can land on snow anywhere.

190
00:09:27,301 --> 00:09:36,140
Airglass is Alaska's leading manufacturer of landing skis for all kinds of aircraft,

191
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:40,320
from military helicopters to single-engine Cessnas.

192
00:09:40,321 --> 00:09:42,960
An engineer named Wes Landis started

193
00:09:42,961 --> 00:09:46,708
the company in 1954 out of his Anchorage garage.

194
00:09:46,709 --> 00:09:50,300
He changed the game for aircraft skis by using

195
00:09:50,301 --> 00:09:54,936
the cutting-edge material Airglass still uses today, fiberglass.

196
00:09:54,937 --> 00:09:56,820
Some of the helicopters we

197
00:09:56,821 --> 00:10:00,220
We make skis for the military hold up to 55,000 pounds.

198
00:10:00,221 --> 00:10:03,920
So the technology has driven how do we make those skis,

199
00:10:03,921 --> 00:10:06,460
how do we make them light, how do we make them functional.

200
00:10:06,461 --> 00:10:07,860
For some of their products,

201
00:10:07,861 --> 00:10:11,640
Airglass uses a new highly durable fiberglass composite

202
00:10:11,641 --> 00:10:13,100
called Twintex.

203
00:10:13,101 --> 00:10:14,840
It's basically indestructible.

204
00:10:14,841 --> 00:10:19,480
It's very flexible, and it's very good for impact resistance,

205
00:10:19,481 --> 00:10:22,680
which is what aircraft skis typically run into,

206
00:10:22,681 --> 00:10:26,440
is a lot of pounding on the snow and ice.

207
00:10:26,441 --> 00:10:29,680
Tom Lawhorn and Joe Witteshek are one of the expert teams

208
00:10:29,681 --> 00:10:31,740
who make each pair of skis by hand.

209
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:35,600
Our largest single cost is labor.

210
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,980
And because it's highly skilled and it's all hand done,

211
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:40,640
we wouldn't have it any other way.

212
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:45,100
Today, the team is making skis

213
00:10:45,101 --> 00:10:47,480
that will fit up to a 4,000-pound Cessna.

214
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:50,180
The process starts at the vacuum mold.

215
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:54,500
The team uses suction to help pull the flexible fabric

216
00:10:54,501 --> 00:10:55,420
tightly into shape.

217
00:10:55,421 --> 00:10:59,420
They layer a total of eight sheets of fiberglass into a mold.

218
00:10:59,421 --> 00:11:03,420
The mold goes into an oven, where the composite bakes

219
00:11:03,421 --> 00:11:06,420
until the layers liquefy and bond together.

220
00:11:06,421 --> 00:11:10,420
As it cools, it solidifies into shape as one rigid piece.

221
00:11:10,421 --> 00:11:14,420
It may not look like it, but this piece of fiberglass

222
00:11:14,421 --> 00:11:18,420
is much stronger than a similar-sized piece of wood,

223
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:23,420
which is what pilots were stuck with before World War II

224
00:11:23,421 --> 00:11:27,160
inspired innovations in landing gear in Alaska.

225
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:29,460
The breakthrough was sparked by an invasion

226
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:32,060
that most of us in the rest of the US tend to forget.

227
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:38,660
In June 1942, Japanese soldiers landed

228
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:42,480
on Alaska's Aleutian Islands, starting a grueling 15-month

229
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:46,780
air war, the first armed invasion of American soil

230
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:49,880
since the War of 1812.

231
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:52,140
With so few runways in Alaska, planes

232
00:11:52,141 --> 00:11:55,840
planes that could land on water soon held the edge.

233
00:11:55,940 --> 00:11:58,740
But their aluminum floats had some problems on snow.

234
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:07,080
When fiberglass became available after the war,

235
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:09,900
Wes Landis was inspired to see what this new material could

236
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,100
do for snow landings.

237
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:14,000
Turns out, a lot.

238
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:20,300
Tom and Joe are now ready to join the top and bottom

239
00:12:20,301 --> 00:12:25,740
and bottom halves of the ski together using a powerful resin.

240
00:12:25,741 --> 00:12:32,460
After a few hours, the ski is ready for its hardware and rigging.

241
00:12:32,461 --> 00:12:35,980
This is a completed LH4000.

242
00:12:35,981 --> 00:12:37,220
The hardware is on it.

243
00:12:37,221 --> 00:12:39,580
It has a completed bottom.

244
00:12:39,581 --> 00:12:42,300
It's been trimmed to the final size.

245
00:12:42,301 --> 00:12:46,100
One of Airglass's unique designs is a hydraulic door

246
00:12:46,101 --> 00:12:49,460
that allows skis to be attached to the landing gear alongside the wheels.

247
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:55,520
In the early days before each flight, pilots had to choose wheels or skis.

248
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,400
Now they can select either option while in flight.

249
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:03,060
A big plus for safety and another example of homegrown innovation.

250
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:08,760
So I do believe made in Alaska, designed in Alaska by people that are flying in Alaska makes a big difference.

251
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:13,760
Coming up...

252
00:13:13,761 --> 00:13:17,760
Nature's unpredictable behavior calls for new tech

253
00:13:17,860 --> 00:13:19,160
to keep pilots alive.

254
00:13:19,260 --> 00:13:22,200
We used to kill one pilot every nine days in Alaska.

255
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:25,500
When Alaska Tech returns on Modern Marvels.

256
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:33,420
We now return to Alaska Tech on Modern Marvels.

257
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:40,120
On this tech journey through America's last frontier,

258
00:13:40,220 --> 00:13:42,760
we've seen some of the unique tools Alaskans use

259
00:13:42,761 --> 00:13:46,260
use to keep moving through relentless cold and ice.

260
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:49,760
But that is just the tip of the iceberg.

261
00:13:49,860 --> 00:13:53,900
Alaskans also face a host of other natural extremes.

262
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Because when Mother Nature hits here, she hits big.

263
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:03,420
Take earthquakes.

264
00:14:06,020 --> 00:14:09,620
Of the 15 strongest quakes ever recorded worldwide,

265
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:12,760
three were in Alaska, including a colossal

266
00:14:12,761 --> 00:14:17,360
earthquake that devastated Anchorage in 1964.

267
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:20,360
It's still the second largest earthquake ever recorded.

268
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:30,820
After the earthquakes, frequently come the killer waves,

269
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:33,420
as the shaking Earth causes underwater landslides

270
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:35,820
that push massive walls of water toward land.

271
00:14:35,821 --> 00:14:40,320
toward land.

272
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:45,800
In 1958, a super tsunami, the largest wave ever known,

273
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:49,000
exploded in Lituya Bay in Alaska's Panhandle.

274
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:55,780
At 1,720 feet, the wave was more than 50 times taller

275
00:14:55,880 --> 00:14:58,440
than the one that hit Japan in 2011.

276
00:14:58,540 --> 00:15:01,340
Fortunately, it struck an unpopulated area,

277
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:04,480
but it uprooted millions of trees on mountaintops that

278
00:15:04,481 --> 00:15:08,480
that still lay bare today.

279
00:15:08,481 --> 00:15:12,420
If that weren't enough, there's also Alaska's more explosive

280
00:15:12,421 --> 00:15:13,220
adversaries.

281
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:18,920
The state has more than 40 active volcanoes.

282
00:15:26,640 --> 00:15:31,440
We have several eruptions per decade compared to the lower

283
00:15:31,441 --> 00:15:34,740
where we have a few eruptions per century.

284
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:36,580
So a lot of action up here.

285
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:39,680
Rick Wessels monitors volcanoes as part of a team

286
00:15:39,780 --> 00:15:42,080
at the Alaska Volcano Observatory.

287
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:43,480
It depends on the volcano.

288
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:45,380
Every volcano is a different beast.

289
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:47,960
So it responds slightly differently to each one.

290
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:50,900
The team uses seismic instruments, gas analysis,

291
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and satellite imagery to detect movement and temperature

292
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:55,900
changes on Alaska's active volcanoes.

293
00:15:55,901 --> 00:15:57,640
I'm not sure this is true.

294
00:15:57,740 --> 00:15:58,840
Yeah, that's good.

295
00:15:58,940 --> 00:16:01,880
One big clue is midwinter in Alaska, of course,

296
00:16:01,980 --> 00:16:05,640
is usually frozen solid at these elevations of these volcanoes.

297
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:06,740
But all of a sudden, we're starting

298
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:09,220
to see liquid water near the top of the volcano.

299
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:11,420
There's water flowing, which doesn't happen in the winter

300
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:14,680
unless something else is going on, like the volcano heating up.

301
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:16,580
That's currently happening on Cleveland

302
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:19,760
Volcano in the Aleutian Islands.

303
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:23,220
Its recent rise in temperature has crews keeping careful watch

304
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:24,120
around the clock.

305
00:16:24,121 --> 00:16:25,120
around the clock.

306
00:16:25,121 --> 00:16:26,120
We can see the energy.

307
00:16:26,121 --> 00:16:32,700
If there were a big eruption cloud in any one of these volcanoes, including the ones

308
00:16:32,701 --> 00:16:35,820
we're focusing on, which is Cleveland Volcano right here, we'd actually be able to see

309
00:16:35,821 --> 00:16:36,820
that develop.

310
00:16:36,821 --> 00:16:39,300
And this data is captured about every 15 minutes.

311
00:16:39,301 --> 00:16:48,960
Monitoring the data daily is crucial, especially for pilots.

312
00:16:48,961 --> 00:16:51,540
Thousands of planes fly these northern skies every day,

313
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:55,060
including large commercial jets with international destinations.

314
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:58,640
A giant ash cloud would endanger them all.

315
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:06,960
For example, in 1987, a redoubt volcano erupted,

316
00:17:07,740 --> 00:17:09,680
about 100 miles from Anchorage's airport.

317
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:14,540
When a KLM 747 jet flew through the ash cloud,

318
00:17:15,180 --> 00:17:16,720
all four engines failed.

319
00:17:16,721 --> 00:17:21,020
The plane fell 14,000 feet before the captain could restart

320
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:23,020
the engines and land.

321
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:25,320
Miraculously, no lives were lost,

322
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:28,660
but the near tragedy cost $80 million in damages.

323
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:35,680
To avoid future accidents, geologist Christy Wallace

324
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:38,480
is seeking a better understanding of ash clouds.

325
00:17:38,580 --> 00:17:40,480
CHRISTY WALLACE, What we know about ash clouds

326
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:45,020
is that they contain glass shards, mineral fragments,

327
00:17:45,021 --> 00:17:46,860
mineral fragments, and rock fragments.

328
00:17:46,861 --> 00:17:49,340
And all of those together can be very abrasive.

329
00:17:49,780 --> 00:17:51,840
But Christie has found that the glass content

330
00:17:51,841 --> 00:17:54,260
is what's most dangerous to aviation.

331
00:17:54,660 --> 00:17:58,480
And that glass can melt at operating temperatures of jet engines.

332
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:01,120
Which can disable a plane in minutes.

333
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:05,280
Christie's study will determine where and when it's safe to fly

334
00:18:05,281 --> 00:18:09,000
after an eruption to avoid unnecessary grounding of air traffic.

335
00:18:09,820 --> 00:18:12,640
That's huge in Alaska, where without planes,

336
00:18:12,641 --> 00:18:14,640
are totally cut off from basic needs.

337
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:26,640
80% of Alaskan villages are not accessible by road.

338
00:18:26,641 --> 00:18:29,640
Planes deliver mail, groceries, and medicine.

339
00:18:29,641 --> 00:18:32,640
They get children to school and the sick to the hospital.

340
00:18:32,641 --> 00:18:35,640
When you dial 911 for an ambulance,

341
00:18:35,641 --> 00:18:40,640
you don't get a car, you get a small airplane.

342
00:18:40,641 --> 00:18:44,640
But Alaska is also one of the most dangerous places to fly.

343
00:18:44,641 --> 00:18:47,640
We used to kill one pilot every nine days in Alaska

344
00:18:47,641 --> 00:18:51,640
and have one major accident every three days.

345
00:18:51,641 --> 00:18:54,640
Challenging flying Alaska due to the terrain

346
00:18:54,641 --> 00:18:56,640
and changing weather conditions

347
00:18:56,641 --> 00:18:59,640
and lack of aviation infrastructure in general.

348
00:18:59,641 --> 00:19:02,640
The standard radar technology relied on by pilots

349
00:19:02,641 --> 00:19:06,640
in the lower 48 has never even existed here.

350
00:19:06,641 --> 00:19:08,640
It's too difficult to install and maintain

351
00:19:08,641 --> 00:19:12,480
to install and maintain in the vast remote areas of the state.

352
00:19:12,481 --> 00:19:15,180
In Alaska, within 10 minutes flying time,

353
00:19:15,181 --> 00:19:18,640
you are out in a place where no man has actually walked

354
00:19:18,641 --> 00:19:22,080
and no buildings or infrastructure is available.

355
00:19:22,081 --> 00:19:24,480
Without radar, until recently, pilots here

356
00:19:24,481 --> 00:19:28,900
had only one tool for navigation, their eyesight.

357
00:19:28,901 --> 00:19:31,040
That's why airplane accident rates in Alaska

358
00:19:31,041 --> 00:19:35,140
were five times higher than in the rest of the country.

359
00:19:35,141 --> 00:19:37,980
To save lives, the Alaska aviation industry

360
00:19:37,981 --> 00:19:41,580
collaborated with the FAA to develop a new state-of-the-art

361
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:44,080
piloting technology.

362
00:19:44,180 --> 00:19:49,020
Called the Capstone Program, this system uses GPS instead of radar

363
00:19:49,120 --> 00:19:52,660
to constantly update pilots on flying conditions.

364
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:55,140
Pilots access the information from a single screen

365
00:19:55,240 --> 00:19:56,740
on their dashboard.

366
00:19:56,840 --> 00:19:59,740
The navigation function keeps them on their flight path,

367
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:02,580
while a continuous weather uplink informs them of Alaska's

368
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:06,540
constantly changing climate conditions.

369
00:20:06,541 --> 00:20:09,880
The traffic feature enables a plane to transmit its position

370
00:20:09,881 --> 00:20:12,080
and receive other aircraft's positions

371
00:20:12,081 --> 00:20:14,940
via GPS and ground stations.

372
00:20:14,941 --> 00:20:18,820
It's a vast improvement to just looking out the window.

373
00:20:18,821 --> 00:20:20,920
The terrain display is a piloting feature

374
00:20:20,921 --> 00:20:24,260
totally unique to Alaska.

375
00:20:24,261 --> 00:20:27,200
A majority of accidents here are referred to as CFIT,

376
00:20:27,201 --> 00:20:30,000
or controlled flight into terrain.

377
00:20:30,001 --> 00:20:31,560
That means crashing into a mountain

378
00:20:31,561 --> 00:20:33,500
you didn't know was there.

379
00:20:33,501 --> 00:20:35,800
This is a good example out the front window.

380
00:20:35,801 --> 00:20:39,100
Even though the sun's out, you can still see snow fields

381
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:41,840
on the bottom with the white clouds a little on top.

382
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:44,340
And you can't really tell where the clouds stop

383
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:47,540
and where the terrain starts.

384
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:50,740
Now the capstone system sees it for you.

385
00:20:50,840 --> 00:20:53,360
Color coding indicates changes in terrain heights

386
00:20:53,460 --> 00:20:55,460
around you as you fly.

387
00:20:55,560 --> 00:20:58,920
Anything green is 2,000 feet below.

388
00:20:59,020 --> 00:21:01,360
Yellow is within 500 feet.

389
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:04,760
And red is at or above your altitude.

390
00:21:04,761 --> 00:21:08,360
So in the red, you're dead.

391
00:21:08,460 --> 00:21:11,460
With constantly updated information at their fingertips,

392
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:14,100
pilots here fly much safer skies.

393
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:16,300
In just one year with the new system,

394
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:20,200
Alaska's aviation accident rate dropped 47%.

395
00:21:20,300 --> 00:21:23,980
It's a far, far safer place than it used to be.

396
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:27,480
By 2020, the FAA plans to equip the lower 48

397
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:29,880
with the second generation of this system,

398
00:21:29,980 --> 00:21:33,380
aptly calling it next gen.

399
00:21:33,381 --> 00:21:35,380
environment in Alaska was a perfect place to test out

400
00:21:35,381 --> 00:21:37,380
the new technology.

401
00:21:37,381 --> 00:21:39,380
If you didn't have an Alaska as a test ground,

402
00:21:39,381 --> 00:21:41,380
then you would have invented one.

403
00:21:43,380 --> 00:21:46,380
Coming up, roads and houses built on ice

404
00:21:46,381 --> 00:21:48,380
can't take the heat.

405
00:21:48,381 --> 00:21:50,380
And this gold digger's got the right tools

406
00:21:50,381 --> 00:21:52,380
to hit pay dirt.

407
00:21:52,381 --> 00:21:56,380
My best days in gold mining was 38 ounces, 15 minutes.

408
00:22:03,380 --> 00:22:09,720
Fish, plants, and insects in Alaska have adapted to survive at temperatures down to 60 below zero,

409
00:22:10,140 --> 00:22:12,360
thanks to antifreezing proteins in their blood.

410
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:17,340
Scientists have begun to isolate these unique proteins and make use of them in new ways,

411
00:22:17,520 --> 00:22:20,560
from making ice cream to de-icing planes.

412
00:22:21,180 --> 00:22:23,560
Alaska Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

413
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:31,500
We now return to Alaska Tech on Modern Marvels.

414
00:22:33,380 --> 00:22:36,220
We've seen how tough a subarctic environment can be

415
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:38,520
on cars and trucks.

416
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:40,120
But what about the roads they drive on?

417
00:22:42,820 --> 00:22:45,520
It turns out the biggest threat to Alaska's highways

418
00:22:45,620 --> 00:22:50,140
lies beneath them in the form of permafrost.

419
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:54,240
Permafrost is ice-rich soil that forms just below the surface

420
00:22:54,340 --> 00:22:57,040
and has been frozen for at least two years.

421
00:22:57,140 --> 00:23:00,340
It covers 25 million kilometers of our planet,

422
00:23:00,440 --> 00:23:02,740
yet most of us have never seen it.

423
00:23:02,741 --> 00:23:05,480
But in Alaska, it can't be escaped.

424
00:23:06,200 --> 00:23:08,440
Permafrost blankets 80% of the state.

425
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:11,900
It lies under most of Alaska's roads and highways,

426
00:23:12,420 --> 00:23:13,660
providing a hard base.

427
00:23:14,360 --> 00:23:16,020
That is, until it melts.

428
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:21,320
Permafrost, as long as it's frozen,

429
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:22,920
it's good stable stuff, right?

430
00:23:23,380 --> 00:23:26,960
But once it thaws, it's water-saturated goo

431
00:23:26,961 --> 00:23:28,060
where the water runs away.

432
00:23:29,700 --> 00:23:32,340
Dr. Matthew Sturm studies permafrost

433
00:23:32,341 --> 00:23:34,700
at the Cold Regions Research and Engineering Lab

434
00:23:34,701 --> 00:23:35,840
near Fairbanks.

435
00:23:36,460 --> 00:23:38,140
The lab is actually a tunnel,

436
00:23:38,500 --> 00:23:39,820
the only one of its kind,

437
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:41,700
providing a rare window

438
00:23:41,701 --> 00:23:44,240
into what Alaskan engineers are up against.

439
00:23:44,860 --> 00:23:49,640
So we know that permafrost is soil, rock, and ice,

440
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:51,900
but often it's hard to see the ice in there.

441
00:23:52,160 --> 00:23:54,420
Here's a good place to see the fact

442
00:23:54,421 --> 00:23:56,240
that there's a lot of ice.

443
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:58,740
There are several types of ice features

444
00:23:58,741 --> 00:24:00,540
that can exist within permafrost,

445
00:24:00,541 --> 00:24:02,880
often layered on top of each other in columns

446
00:24:02,980 --> 00:24:05,780
that can be up to 1,000 feet deep.

447
00:24:05,880 --> 00:24:10,480
So the column we're looking at here, it's mostly water.

448
00:24:10,580 --> 00:24:13,280
You wouldn't build a road on a lake that was frozen

449
00:24:13,380 --> 00:24:18,300
and expect it to survive that spring, right?

450
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:20,500
That's information Alaskans could have used

451
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:23,200
before building their highway system,

452
00:24:23,300 --> 00:24:25,900
since most of their roads run through the most problematic

453
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:30,540
permafrost region, creating relentless road hazards.

454
00:24:30,541 --> 00:24:35,540
Mike Coffey heads up the Alaska DOT maintenance division.

455
00:24:35,640 --> 00:24:37,340
His massive road repair operation

456
00:24:37,440 --> 00:24:39,140
comes with a hefty price tag.

457
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:42,080
MIKE COFFEY, Alaska DOT maintenance division.

458
00:24:42,180 --> 00:24:43,080
We spend $10 million to $12 million

459
00:24:43,180 --> 00:24:45,480
because that's what we have available to do the work.

460
00:24:45,580 --> 00:24:51,100
If we had $20 million, we'd be spending $20 million.

461
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:54,100
Year after year, crews spend the short summer months

462
00:24:54,200 --> 00:24:57,840
patching cracks and gaping holes in the roads

463
00:24:57,841 --> 00:25:02,420
and leveling out what they refer to as the roller coaster effect.

464
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:05,320
It's a never-ending game of catch-up.

465
00:25:05,980 --> 00:25:10,080
We're basically, you know, the Band-Aid on the big problem.

466
00:25:10,980 --> 00:25:15,320
So the big goal is to find new ways to stop the thawing before it starts.

467
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:19,260
So the interesting thing for engineers in Alaska

468
00:25:19,261 --> 00:25:21,580
is we're basically in the refrigeration business.

469
00:25:22,120 --> 00:25:26,920
We try to keep the ground frozen to keep the structure on top of it stable.

470
00:25:26,921 --> 00:25:29,960
So this may look like a big pile of rocks,

471
00:25:30,060 --> 00:25:32,120
but it's actually a state-of-the-art air conditioning

472
00:25:32,220 --> 00:25:34,560
system for the ground.

473
00:25:34,660 --> 00:25:36,960
It's called an air convection embankment,

474
00:25:37,060 --> 00:25:38,560
or ACE rock system.

475
00:25:38,660 --> 00:25:44,580
The concept of the ACE rock is it's all 6 to 12-inch rock,

476
00:25:44,680 --> 00:25:46,580
and there's a lot of voids in it.

477
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:50,080
And so what it allows is air circulation

478
00:25:50,180 --> 00:25:52,080
during the winter time.

479
00:25:52,180 --> 00:25:54,580
The weight of the road causes the ground below it

480
00:25:54,680 --> 00:25:55,680
to heat up.

481
00:25:55,681 --> 00:25:58,220
rather than trapping all that heat in the soil,

482
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:01,160
the hot air can now rise up through the voids between the

483
00:26:01,260 --> 00:26:05,860
rocks, while the cold air from above is drawn into the ground,

484
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:08,320
keeping the permafrost frozen below.

485
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:13,000
So we have to try and be proactive and learn on our own

486
00:26:13,100 --> 00:26:14,200
because it's our problem.

487
00:26:18,900 --> 00:26:20,900
Permafrost's unstable surface

488
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,200
presents similar problems for Alaska's construction

489
00:26:23,300 --> 00:26:25,680
industry.

490
00:26:25,681 --> 00:26:28,780
Building projects, like this hospital in Nome,

491
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:31,180
face a sobering challenge.

492
00:26:31,280 --> 00:26:33,560
A large building can generate enough heat

493
00:26:33,660 --> 00:26:37,500
to accelerate the permafrost's thawing significantly.

494
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:39,800
That means buildings often buckle and cave

495
00:26:39,900 --> 00:26:41,800
into collapsed ground.

496
00:26:41,900 --> 00:26:43,100
The solution?

497
00:26:43,200 --> 00:26:46,240
Place the building on pilings to keep these monster heat

498
00:26:46,340 --> 00:26:51,900
sources off the ground and allow for circulation in between.

499
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:55,680
40 to 60 foot pilings are driven deep into the soil.

500
00:26:55,681 --> 00:26:58,680
This pile is vertical.

501
00:26:58,780 --> 00:27:01,280
It's essentially what we call a friction pile.

502
00:27:01,380 --> 00:27:04,420
It's held in the ground by the friction of the ground.

503
00:27:04,520 --> 00:27:07,220
But friction only works when the ground is frozen,

504
00:27:07,320 --> 00:27:09,420
and eventually the surrounding permafrost soil

505
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:11,620
will start to thaw.

506
00:27:11,720 --> 00:27:15,340
They got to bring in the big guns.

507
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:17,140
So we don't need a big area frozen,

508
00:27:17,240 --> 00:27:19,740
but we need the area around that pile frozen.

509
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:21,780
So that's why you'll see a thermal siphon

510
00:27:21,880 --> 00:27:24,240
adjacent to every pile.

511
00:27:24,241 --> 00:27:32,860
A thermosyphon is a tube driven into the ground, in this case near each piling.

512
00:27:32,861 --> 00:27:37,200
Liquid carbon dioxide fills the bottom of the tube, which absorbs heat from the earth

513
00:27:37,201 --> 00:27:39,180
